{%- from '_macros.jinja' import cmd with context -%}
---
description: Implement the current feature one phase at a time, ticking tasks.md and running the verification gate at each phase boundary
argument-hint: <spec-dir-name> (optional; defaults to the most recent development/work/* directory)
---

You are carrying out the implementation phase of a feature.

1. Identify the target spec directory.
   - If `$ARGUMENTS` is provided, use `development/work/$ARGUMENTS/`.
   - Otherwise, use the most recently modified directory under
     `development/work/`.
2. Read `spec.md`, `plan.md`, and `tasks.md` in full. If `plan.md` is
   missing or empty, stop and tell the user to run `/plan` first.
3. If the plan touches an unfamiliar area of the codebase, run an
   `explorer` subagent pass first to summarise the relevant code
   paths, and put the summary in `scratch.md` for the Developer to
   read. (The Developer is itself a subagent and cannot spawn the
   `explorer` subagent on its own.)
4. Delegate the work to the **developer** subagent
   (`.agents/subagents/developer.md`). In the delegation prompt,
   tell it to read `scratch.md` first if you populated it in
   step 3 — that is where the explorer summary lives, and the
   Developer's required-reading rule already covers it when present.
   The developer will then:
   - Work one phase at a time, writing tests first where the plan
     calls for behaviour change.
   - Tick `tasks.md` checkboxes in the same commit as the code change.
   - Keep `report.md` current: deviations, abandoned approaches, and
     `DECISION-PENDING:` escalations are recorded when they happen.
   - Run `{{ cmd('verify') }}` at every phase boundary.
   - Stop at the end of each phase and hand off to `/verify`
     (Reviewer) before starting the next.
5. If the developer hands back a **search request** — an
   `EXPLORER-REQUEST: [phase <n>] …` line in `scratch.md` naming what it
   needs summarised (it cannot spawn the `explorer` subagent itself, see
   step 3) — run the `explorer` pass and append its answer to
   `scratch.md` as `EXPLORER-FINDING: [phase <n>] <the question> →
   <answer>`, carrying the same phase tag and **keeping the explorer's
   `path:LINE` citations**: the developer handed the search back because
   it could not do it inline, and an uncited sentence makes it redo the
   search. If the explorer replies with a clarifying question instead of
   an answer, narrow the request from the developer's own words and
   re-run it — do not append a question as a finding, and do not spend a
   round on a non-answer. Then re-invoke the developer, telling it to
   read `scratch.md` first and passing back the round number it gave
   you; it starts with fresh context and picks the summary up there.
   Cap this at **three rounds per phase**, and that round number is the
   count. If the developer gave none, tally the requests tagged with the
   phase being built, but treat the tally as an upper bound —
   `scratch.md` spans the whole feature and outlives any `/verify`
   rework of the phase. At three, stop and put it to the user, quoting
   the three requests rather than declaring the phase too wide on a
   count you cannot back.
6. Two other hand-backs stop the developer mid-phase, and neither is a
   phase boundary:
   - a `DECISION-PENDING:` line in `report.md` — put that question to
     the user, then re-invoke the developer with the answer so it can
     finish the phase, and add the decision's row to the register
     (`development/adr/README.md`).
   - a `PLAN-REVISION:` line in `scratch.md` — the plan itself is
     wrong, so hand back to `/plan` (Architect) with that line, not to
     `/verify`. Once the plan is revised, re-run `/build`.
7. When the developer reports a phase complete, **stop** and ask the
   user to run `/verify` before proceeding. Do not auto-start the
   next phase.

Never silently skip a failing test, edit anything under `*/generated/`,
or run destructive Git. If the plan turns out to be wrong, hand back
to `/plan` (Architect) rather than silently re-planning.
